.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_FREE_ENTRY 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_free_entry \- free an entry back to the ranged pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

ccol_r_mempool_free_entry(rmp, entry);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns an allocated entry to the sub-pool that owns it. Macro wrapper
around _ccol_r_mempool_free_entry() that sets the caller's pointer to
NULL.

The ranged pool is named by the caller rather than derived from the
entry. Which of its sub-pools owns the entry is resolved from the
address, so the caller never has to track that. Free an entry to the
ranged pool it was allocated from; passing a different one is a caller
error and is fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp    Ranged pool the entry was allocated from
entry  Entry to free (will be set to NULL after freeing)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(1) in the number of sub-pools
Works for entries from any sub-pool in the ranged pool
Also works for dynamically allocated entries (fallback)
Safe to call with a NULL entry; a NULL pool is a caller error and fatal
Performs corruption detection via assertions

Which sub-pool owns the entry is resolved from its address; see
ccol_mempool_free_entry(3) and ccol_r_mempool_create(3).
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Sub-pools are locked independently, so frees of different size classes
never contend with one another, and within a tier that maintains
per-thread caches the common path takes no lock at all. An entry freed
by one thread may be handed out to another.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_free_entry (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_alloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create (3)
